          "tool_use_id": "toolu_0138vATQCsvw86AGm6pntE2r",
          "tool_name": "write_file",
          "input_json": "{\u0022path\u0022: \u0022concepts/autoscaling.md\u0022, \u0022content\u0022: \u0022---\\ntype: Concept\\ntitle: Autoscaling\\ndescription: Automatically adjusting the number of running replicas of a service based on observed load.\\ntimestamp: 2026-01-14T00:00:00Z\\ntags:\\n  - concept/Autoscaling\\n  - concept/Kubernetes\\n  - tech/Kubernetes\\nconfidence: medium\\nconfidence_reason: \\\u0022Two sources provide consistent information; no significant corroboration yet.\\\u0022\\n---\\n\\nAutoscaling adjusts replica count for a service automatically instead of requiring a\\nhuman to resize it manually. The controller observes some signal \u2014 CPU/memory\\nutilization, queue depth, or a custom metric \u2014 and computes a desired replica count on\\nan interval.\\n\\n## Kubernetes Horizontal Pod Autoscaler (HPA)\\n\\nKubernetes\u0027s Horizontal Pod Autoscaler scales replica count based on observed CPU/memory \\nutilization or custom metrics. It polls the metrics API on an interval, compares against \\na target, and computes a desired replica count. To avoid flapping (rapid oscillation \\nbetween scaling up and down due to metric fluctuations near the target threshold), HPA \\nuses a **stabilization window** that dampens rapid scaling decisions.\\n\\nHPA controls only pod replicas. It does not provision new nodes \u2014 that is the responsibility \\nof the [[Cluster Autoscaler]], a related but separate controller that runs at the node level.\\n\\n## Citations\\n\\n[^1]: Kubernetes HPA documentation and behavior (eval://steering-adoption/sample-02)\\n\u0022}"
        }
      ],
